course: dictionary
lesson: 13447
title: The hostess
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hostess is a woman who invites guests and looks after them. You might meet one at a party in someone's home, at a restaurant, or on a TV game show. Look at the words around the word to find its meaning: «The hostess made sure every guest had a drink.» Guests and drinks tell you she is looking after people.
explain: Hostess has more than one meaning, so check the context. «The hostess showed us to our table by the window.» Here a hostess greets people in a restaurant. «The game show hostess waved to the cheering crowd.» Here she presents the show.
fact: A hostess is a woman who invites guests and looks after them.
fact: In a restaurant, a hostess greets people and shows them to their table.
fact: A game show hostess presents the show.
fact: Old use: a hostess was a woman who looked after passengers on a plane. Today we call this job a flight attendant.
fact: Many people now say «host» for any gender, whether the person is a man or a woman.
exercise: «The hostess greeted every guest with a smile.» What is a hostess? | A large woollen rug | A bird that sings at night | *A woman who invites guests and looks after them | A type of cake
exercise: «The hostess at the restaurant led us to a table by the window.» What job does the hostess do here? | *Shows people to their tables | Cooks the food in the kitchen | Washes the dishes | Drives the delivery van
exercise: «The hostess served tea and cake to her guests.» What did the hostess serve? | *Tea and cake | Bricks and sand | Stones from the garden | Dinner for a dog
test: «The game show hostess smiled and waved at the crowd.» What does the game show hostess do? | Gives out the prizes to the crowd | Works the cameras | *Presents the show | Sells tickets at the door
test: Old books say «The hostess brought me a drink on the plane.» What did the hostess do on the plane? | Sold tickets at the gate | *Looked after the passengers | Flew the plane | Cleaned the engines
exam: Which word do many people use today instead of hostess to cover any gender? | hostel | hostile | guest | *host
